WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:02.574
Here is an incredible thought.

00:00:02.574 --> 00:00:08.154
You are the living ancestor of an unbroken
chain of lives stretching from you, through

00:00:08.154 --> 00:00:12.344
your mother and your grandmother all the way
back through the complete history of life

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:18.474
on planet Earth to the very first life forms
that appeared around four billion years ago.

00:00:18.474 --> 00:00:21.464
Isn’t that mind blowing!

00:00:21.464 --> 00:00:25.534
And the fact that you exist proves that this
is true.

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:30.454
Combined, of course, with our understanding
of evolutionary history where life on Earth

00:00:30.454 --> 00:00:35.734
has evolved into more complex forms over time
due to different pressures from the environment

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:39.173
and competition with other animals.

00:00:39.173 --> 00:00:44.414
Try and imagine all of your direct ancestors,
all of those individual creatures that managed

00:00:44.414 --> 00:00:52.044
to survive birth, avoided being eaten or dying
from disease and getting enough food for long

00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:57.593
enough so that they could rear their young,
over and over and over again until it all

00:00:57.593 --> 00:00:59.854
ended up with you.

00:00:59.854 --> 00:01:04.304
Every single life form in that chain managed
to do that.

00:01:04.304 --> 00:01:08.594
Now I’ve oversimplified a little bit by
just talking about a chain of life, in reality

00:01:08.594 --> 00:01:13.524
there is a big web of lives going back into
the past, but just to keep things simple you

00:01:13.524 --> 00:01:17.804
can think about a chain of all of your ancestral
mums.

00:01:17.804 --> 00:01:22.684
Imagine your mum, and then her mum and her
mum and so on and so on going back through

00:01:22.684 --> 00:01:28.494
all of human civilisation back to when we
were human hunter gatherers in Africa.

00:01:28.494 --> 00:01:34.004
Direct ancestors of yours survived a super-volcano
that dimmed the Sun for 6 whole years, and

00:01:34.004 --> 00:01:39.464
glacial periods where the Earth turned cold
and dry, and the total global population of

00:01:39.464 --> 00:01:46.004
humans is estimated to have been about 10,000
people and may have been even lower.

00:01:46.004 --> 00:01:51.034
Then back through the ages when our ancestors
were hunted by sabre toothed cats in the Savannahs

00:01:51.034 --> 00:01:56.924
of Africa and lived in a world shared with
giant gorilla horses and giant sloths the

00:01:56.924 --> 00:01:59.244
size of elephants.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:04.093
Then through our common ancestors with the
great apes, monkeys through to the animals

00:02:04.093 --> 00:02:09.454
that survived the impact of the meteorite
that killed off most of the dinosaurs.

00:02:09.454 --> 00:02:14.274
The last common ancestor of humans and mice
was 100 million years ago, and all of the

00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:20.784
mammals including you came from the first
mammals which were small shrew like animals.

00:02:20.784 --> 00:02:25.744
Then back through earlier animals to the common
ancestor with dinosaurs and modern reptiles

00:02:25.744 --> 00:02:26.744
and birds.

00:02:26.744 --> 00:02:31.764
You are related to the first reptiles which
evolved from amphibians which evolved from

00:02:31.764 --> 00:02:36.284
fish and they came from even earlier vertebrates.

00:02:36.284 --> 00:02:41.394
And these came from the first animals to have
a heart and circulatory system, and then worms

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:46.584
with the first bilateral symmetry, the first
animals with nerves and muscles, to sponge

00:02:46.584 --> 00:02:49.884
like creatures with the first different kinds
of tissues.

00:02:49.884 --> 00:02:55.794
And before multicellular organisms there were
just single cells, complex cells, oxygen breathing

00:02:55.794 --> 00:03:00.603
cells, first cells like bacteria, back to
the very first lifeforms that turned from

00:03:00.603 --> 00:03:04.564
mixtures of chemicals to self replicating
molecules.

00:03:04.564 --> 00:03:10.954
You come from an unbroken chain of lives that
span that unimaginable time, you are connected

00:03:10.954 --> 00:03:17.144
to eons and eons of creatures all fighting
for survival and winning.

00:03:17.144 --> 00:03:21.924
And I think that is so incredible, it completely
blows my mind.

00:03:21.924 --> 00:03:25.914
Conclusion
What can you conclude from this thought?

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:33.774
Well what I take from it is that this chain
of life doesn’t just apply to us, is true

00:03:33.774 --> 00:03:36.644
for every life from on the planet.

00:03:36.644 --> 00:03:43.994
The Earth is like a living museum of all of
the life we know about so far in the entire

00:03:43.994 --> 00:03:44.994
Universe.

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:50.224
So I think it is important that we preserve
as much of this biodiversity as possible.

00:03:50.224 --> 00:03:52.834
Thats what I think but perhaps you have a
different take, I’d be interested to hear

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:53.834
you perspectives.

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:55.664
And, as always, posers are available, check
the link in the description below.

